{
  "slug": "adjetivos",
  "number": 13,
  "part": 3,
  "title": "Adjectives",
  "tagline": "Plural, position, bel, nouvel, vieil, adjectives that change meaning, and comparisons.",
  "goal": "Make adjectives plural, know whether they go before or after the noun, use bel, nouvel and vieil, understand adjectives that change meaning with their position and compare things.",
  "minutes": 12,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "femenino",
      "title": "Recap: the feminine",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Unlike English, French adjectives agree with the noun [[2]]: *un pull noir* → *une robe noire*. And the irregular ones: *beau → belle, vieux → vieille, blanc → blanche, heureux → heureuse*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "plural",
      "title": "The plural",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Rule",
            "Singular → plural"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "General: **+ s**",
              "*petit → petits · petite → petites*"
            ],
            [
              "Ends in **-s or -x**: no change",
              "*français → français · heureux → heureux*"
            ],
            [
              "**-eau → -eaux**",
              "*beau → beaux · nouveau → nouveaux*"
            ],
            [
              "**-al → -aux**",
              "*normal → normaux · génial → géniaux*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "The plural -s and -x are almost never pronounced; you only see them in writing. In the feminine, *-ale* becomes *-ales*: *des idées géniales*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "posicion",
      "title": "Before or after?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**The general rule is the opposite of English:** in French the adjective goes **after** the noun. «A red car» = *une voiture rouge*; «an interesting book» = *un livre intéressant*."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "But a small group of short, very common adjectives goes **before**, as in English. They fall into four ideas, often remembered as **BAGS**:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Idea",
            "Adjectives",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**B**eauty",
              "*beau, joli*",
              "*une jolie maison*"
            ],
            [
              "**A**ge",
              "*jeune, vieux, nouveau*",
              "*un vieux quartier*"
            ],
            [
              "**G**oodness",
              "*bon, mauvais, gentil*",
              "*un bon restaurant*"
            ],
            [
              "**S**ize",
              "*grand, petit, gros, long*",
              "*une petite ville*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "If you're not sure, put it after: that's right for most adjectives, including colours and nationalities. Ordinal numbers go before too: *le premier jour, la deuxième fois*."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "When a plural adjective comes first, careful French turns *des* into *de*: *de beaux châteaux, de bonnes idées*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bel",
      "title": "Bel, nouvel, vieil",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before a **masculine singular noun starting with a vowel or silent h**, three adjectives change form:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Normal",
            "Before a vowel",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*beau*",
              "*bel*",
              "*un {bel} homme, un {bel} appartement*"
            ],
            [
              "*nouveau*",
              "*nouvel*",
              "*un {nouvel} ordinateur*"
            ],
            [
              "*vieux*",
              "*vieil*",
              "*un {vieil} ami*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Same reason as *cet* [[3]] and *mon amie* [[8]]: avoiding two vowels clashing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sentido",
      "title": "Adjectives that change meaning",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Some adjectives **change meaning** depending on where they go:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Before the noun",
            "After the noun"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*un grand homme*: a great man",
              "*un homme grand*: a tall man"
            ],
            [
              "*mon ancien professeur*: my former teacher",
              "*une maison ancienne*: an old (antique) house"
            ],
            [
              "*un pauvre homme*: a poor man (to be pitied)",
              "*un homme pauvre*: a man with no money"
            ],
            [
              "*ma propre chambre*: my own room",
              "*une chambre propre*: a clean room"
            ],
            [
              "*un cher ami*: a dear friend",
              "*un sac cher*: an expensive bag"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "colores",
      "title": "Colours",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Masculine",
            "Feminine"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*blanc*",
              "*blanche*"
            ],
            [
              "*noir*",
              "*noire*"
            ],
            [
              "*vert*",
              "*verte*"
            ],
            [
              "*bleu*",
              "*bleue*"
            ],
            [
              "*gris*",
              "*grise*"
            ],
            [
              "*violet*",
              "*violette*"
            ],
            [
              "*rouge, jaune, rose*",
              "the same (they already end in -e)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "Colours named after a thing (a fruit, a stone…) are **invariable**: they don't change in the feminine or plural. *des chaussures orange · des yeux marron* (brown eyes). *rose* is the exception: it does agree (*des fleurs roses*)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparar",
      "title": "Comparing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "French has no -er or -est endings: everything is built with *plus* (more) and *moins* (less)."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Comparison",
            "Structure",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "More … than",
              "*plus* + adjective + *que*",
              "*Le train est {plus} rapide {que} le bus.*"
            ],
            [
              "Less … than",
              "*moins* + adjective + *que*",
              "*Le bus est {moins} rapide {que} le train.*"
            ],
            [
              "As … as",
              "*aussi* + adjective + *que*",
              "*Paul est {aussi} grand {que} Marie.*"
            ],
            [
              "The most …",
              "*le / la / les plus* + adjective",
              "*C'est {la plus} belle ville du monde.*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Bon → meilleur",
          "text": "Like «good → better», never «plus bon»: *Ce vin est meilleur que l'autre. · C'est le meilleur restaurant du quartier.*"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
